ParticleAHFinderX
=================

ParticleAHFinderX is a CarpetX apparent-horizon finder under development. Its
surface points will use persistent AMReX particle ownership, while a logical
cell-centered latitude/longitude view supplies the angular stencil for a
portable GPU hyperbolic-relaxation solve.

Once a surface is created, its AMReX particles and device allocation live
across searches and physical timesteps. Searches update the existing population
in place; regrids redistribute it. Populations are created only for a new
surface and removed only on explicit retirement or shutdown.

The current thorn validates its configuration, attaches persistent pure-SoA
containers to CarpetX, creates configured and provisional angular surfaces with
portable AMReX kernels, and redistributes the same particles after regrids. Its
narrow checked CarpetX adapter gathers live ADM fields with portable
trilinear/tensor-cubic interpolation and analytic derivatives. The device path
evaluates angular derivatives and outgoing expansion, evolves the surface with
internal SSPRK3 or RK4 pseudo-time relaxation, tracks centers, manages bounded
common-surface candidates, writes diagnostics/particle output/checkpoints, and
optionally exports a bounded compatibility surface. A surface repeatedly
outside a non-periodic physical domain is explicitly retired and its particle
population is compacted; its stable ID is never reused.

Surface resolution
------------------

For surface `i`, set `ntheta[i]` and `nphi[i]`. The version-one topology
requires `nphi = 2*ntheta`, and its particle count is exactly
`ntheta*nphi`. Examples are 512 particles at `16x32`, the default 2,048 at
`32x64`, and 8,192 at `64x128`. Resolution is selected at surface birth and is
not runtime-steerable: changing it later requires an explicit remeshing
lifecycle operation that preserves lineage and defines new stable particle
IDs.

The particle count is independent of the mesh interpolation stencil. The
cubic primitive reads one local `4x4x4` (64-sample) mesh stencil per surface
particle; it does not create interpolation particles.

Time integration
----------------

ParticleAHFinderX runs only at synchronized analysis times, after the physical
evolution step has completed. It therefore works independently of the method
selected by `ODESolvers`, including explicit, implicit, and IMEX methods. The
surface finder has its own `relaxation_integrator` parameter; `ssprk3` and
`rk4` are portable GPU implementations and operate only in pseudo-time while
the physical metric and curvature slice is frozen.

Center policies
---------------

Every configured surface has a manual center and radius. The center policy can
also use generic Cactus real scalars, self-centroid history, or a combination.
For PunctureTracker, configure for example:

  ParticleAHFinderX::center_policy[0] = "puncture_tracker_displacement"
  ParticleAHFinderX::external_center_x[0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_x[0]"
  ParticleAHFinderX::external_center_y[0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_y[0]"
  ParticleAHFinderX::external_center_z[0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_z[0]"

Before the first successful solve this policy uses the current puncture. Later
it uses `C_AH(last) + P(now) - P(last)`, retaining the measured AH/puncture
offset while following the puncture's motion. The external provider supplies a
center, not a radius. The initial radius is a parameter and later radii come
from successful surface history.

Residual profiles
-----------------

The compatibility profile uses loose residual defaults
`(M*L2, M*Linf)=(1e-2,1e-5)`. The dynamic-tracking profile uses
`(1e-5,1e-4)`. Select `custom` for independently configured positive values.

See the repository `HANDOFF.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `docs/` directory for the full
architecture, implementation sequence, and validation requirements.
